554, "span": {"begin": 399, "end": 402}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:1111"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "CHK1", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9957990050315857, "span": {"begin": 420, "end": 424}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:1111"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "CHK1", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9969872832298279, "span": {"begin": 426, "end": 430}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:11200"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "CHK2", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.99626225233078, "span": {"begin": 435, "end": 439}}, {"id": ["CUI-less"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "CDC25", "obj": "DNA", "prob": 0.5892816781997681, "span": {"begin": 452, "end": 457}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:995", "5923"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "CDC25 phosphatases", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9954941272735596, "span": {"begin": 452, "end": 470}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:983"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "cyclin-dependent kinases", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9993100166320801, "span": {"begin": 501, "end": 525}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:7157"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "p53", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9974608421325684, "span": {"begin": 583, "end": 586}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:644914"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "p21", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9950993657112122, "span": {"begin": 610, "end": 613}}, {"id": ["CUI-less"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "human cells", "obj": "cell_type", "prob": 0.7700247764587402, "span": {"begin": 1087, "end": 1098}}, {"id": ["NCBITaxon:9606"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "human", "obj": "species", "prob": 0.9401280283927917, "span": {"begin": 1087, "end": 1092}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:1523", "10491", "9595"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "Cas9", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9916816353797913, "span": {"begin": 1109, "end": 1113}}, {"id": ["CUI-less"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "eukaryotes", "obj": "cell_type", "prob": 0.5067552924156189, "span": {"begin": 1546, "end": 1556}}], "text": "The biological plausibility of this KER is high. DNA strand breaks are recognized by DNA damage response pathways. DSBs are sensed by the MRN complex (MRE11&ndash;RAD50&ndash;NBS1), which recruits and activates ATM; ATM phosphorylates H2AX (forming &gamma;H2AX) and CHK2 and contributes to p53 stabilization. SSBs and stalled replication forks generate RPA-coated single-stranded DNA that activates ATR and its effector CHK1. CHK1 and CHK2 inhibit the CDC25 phosphatases, which prevents activation of cyclin-dependent kinases and causes arrest at G1/S, in S phase, or at G2/M, while p53-dependent induction of p21 maintains prolonged arrest when damage persists (Hartwell and Weinert, 1989; O&#39;Connell et al., 2000; Cuddihy and O&#39;Connell, 2003; Jackson and Bartek, 2009; Ciccia and Elledge, 2010). This relationship is canonical knowledge in DNA damage response biology and is the basis of the DNA damage checkpoint concept.  Mechanistically, the response depends on the damage load and the cell-cycle context. A limited number of DSBs is sufficient to activate the checkpoint in human cells: a single Cas9-induced DSB delayed cell cycle progression, and G1-to-S progression decreased in a graded manner as the number of breaks increased (van den Berg et al., 2018). In non-mammalian taxa, DNA-damage-induced cell cycle responses have been described in green algae, although the effectors and the dependence on cell-cycle phase differ from those in animals (Hlavov&aacute; et al., 2011). The KER is therefore biologically plausible across eukaryotes but conditional on lesion type, damage persistence, cell-cycle phase, and checkpoint competence.  &nbsp;", "timestamp": "Tue Oct 13 15:52:05 +0000 2026"}